    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\nWriting to object attributes of extension types uses decref before assignment, leaving the attribute value in an invalid state for a split second:\n```c\n  __Pyx_GOTREF((PyObject *)__pyx_v_obj->attr);\n  __Pyx_DECREF((PyObject *)__pyx_v_obj->attr);  // <- dead reference !\n  __pyx_v_obj->attr = __pyx_v_value;\n```\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\n\n```cython\ncdef class Value:\n    pass\n\ncdef class Ext:\n    cdef Value attr\n\ndef update():\n    x = Ext()\n    value = Value()\n    x.attr = value  # <-\n```\n\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\nWe should use `ExprNode.generate_decref_set()` to generate \"read-update-decref\" ordering. That's still not completely safe (race condition between read and update) but avoids leaving the potentially dead reference in the attribute before updating it. Thus, concurrent updates would stay unsafe, but an update with only concurrent reads would become safe, which brings it to a reasonable and cheap level of safety that is user controllable.\n\n### OS\n\n_No response_\n\n### Python version\n\n_No response_\n\n### Cython version\n\nmaster\n\n### Additional context\n\n_No response_",
